        "source":  "NVIDIA AI Blog",
        "url":  "https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/open-secure-ai-alliance-contributions/",
        "date":  "2026-08-04",
        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "The Open Secure AI Alliance ΓÇö a coalition of over 120 organizations including NVIDIA, Cisco, and CrowdStrike ΓÇö has published the Shared AI Findings Exchange (SAFE) guidelines to standardize security practices for agentic AI systems. The guidelines establish mechanisms for confidential incident data collection, cross-organization threat intelligence sharing, and evidence-based security recommendations. Alliance members are contributing open-source tools across the full AI security stack, spanning identity controls, model harnesses, security models, and resilience frameworks.",
        "execSummary":  "The SAFE guidelines represent the most concrete industry-led attempt to date to create a shared security standard for agentic AI ΓÇö 120 organizations agreeing on incident reporting and threat intelligence exchange is a meaningful signal that AI security is being taken seriously at the ecosystem level, not just by individual vendors. For enterprise security and AI governance leaders, this coalition\u0027s output is worth tracking as it could become a de facto compliance baseline before formal regulation catches up to agentic deployment realities. The open-source tooling contributions lower the barrier for enterprise security teams to evaluate and adopt AI-specific defenses. Note that NVIDIA has commercial interests in the AI infrastructure underlying these deployments, so the alliance\u0027s framing favors open, NVIDIA-compatible architectures.",
