        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "The article argues that compliance and regulatory readiness should be integrated into technology architecture from the outset, not bolted on afterward. Three strategies are presented: controls-by-design principles, cross-functional team alignment around shared accountability, and leveraging specialized compliance partnerships. The author contends that early regulatory integration accelerates rather than impedes innovation.",
        "execSummary":  "As AI regulation matures globally, organizations that treat compliance as an architectural input ΓÇö not a post-launch audit ΓÇö are converting that readiness into speed and market access. The controls-by-design approach described here is directly applicable to AI deployment in regulated industries like financial services, healthcare, and government-facing customer operations. Enterprise leaders building AI-powered support or operations should embed compliance touchpoints into their agent design cycles now, before regulatory pressure forces costly retrofits. The caveat: cross-functional alignment is cited as critical, meaning governance cannot live solely in IT or Legal.",
