        "title":  "These startups are chasing the next big thing in LLMs",
        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/08/10/1141511/these-startups-are-chasing-the-next-big-thing-in-llms/",
        "date":  "2026-08-10",
        "theme":  "Models",
        "summary":  "The article examines startups developing post-transformer alternatives to address LLMs\u0027 computational bottlenecks. Four approaches are profiled: sparse attention mechanisms (Subquadratic), retention-based systems (Manifest AI), hybrid liquid neural networks (Liquid AI), diffusion-based text generation (Inception), and state space models for non-linguistic reasoning (Pathway). Each approach targets a specific limitation of the dominant transformer architecture.",
        "execSummary":  "The transformer architecture that powers every major enterprise AI platform today has structural computational limits, and a new generation of startups is betting the next wave of LLMs will look fundamentally different. For enterprise AI leaders, the strategic implication is that model infrastructure investments made today may depreciate faster than planned if post-transformer architectures mature within a two-to-three year horizon. Organizations with large model fine-tuning investments or tightly coupled infrastructure should begin scenario planning for architectural transitions. The key caveat: none of these approaches has yet demonstrated the general-purpose scale of GPT-4-class transformers; this is an emerging research frontier, not an imminent product shift.",
